XTHE LITTLE DOG:
X
XTo me, the most interesting (although by far not the most useful)
feature Xof hack is the little dog, and the way tame creatures work
in general in hack. XIf the user wishes to try to make use of his
pet, he should be aware of its Xabilities, limitations and its
intelligence (or lack thereof). X
XThe dog is friendly towards the user, and will tend to follow him
around Xthe dungeon. However, the dog, like the user, requires food
to live. The dog Xcan eat every non-poisonous substance safely, and
will not eat tainted meat. XUnder normal circumstances, the dog will
not eat food rations, fruit and Xother "human" foods.
X
XThe dog goes through three stages of development: little dog, dog and
Xlarge dog. In fact, the dog actually goes through more, but they are
divided Xequally into these three classes. All dogs may attack twice
per turn, and move Xtwo spaces per turn (i.e. they are hasted). Dogs
will do about 1-6 points of Xdamage per hit, so they can do 2-12
points of damage per round. A hasted dog Xcan be quite vicious.
X
XThe dog improves by gaining experience from killing monsters, just
as the Xcharacter does. The dog, however, is limited to level 6, and
about 85 hp, so Xthe dog cannot become as powerful as the character.
However, initially, Xthe dog can be much better than the character at
killing creatures, and so can Xbe useful for low-level characters as
a guard dog. X
XIf the user goes up or down levels for any reason, then if the dog
is on Xa space adjacent to the character, he will change levels with
the user. But Xif the dog is not on an adjacent space, he is stranded
on the old level. XWhen a dog becomes stranded and the hacker does
not return for a while, he Xbecomes wild. The user may also find
other wild dogs in the dungeon. A wild Xdog will attack the user like
any other monster. However, the dog will be Xtamed if the user throws
any food (except fruit) at the dog. The dog will then X"devour" the
food, and is from then on the pet of the user. Note, also, that Xthe
user is not limited to one pet, but may tame as many pets as he
wishes. X
XA pet has other abilities as well. The first is that the pet will
tend Xnot to walk over cursed items unless the item has food on it
which the dog Xintends to eat. This gives the user a cheap test to
see if an item is cursed. XIn addition, the dog will pick up items
that he finds, and bring them to the Xuser.
X
XFinally, the dog will stay closer to the user if the user has a tripe
Xration.
X
XGHOST LEVELS:
X
XOccasionally, when a character dies, hack will save the level he died
Xon for future use. This level is called a ghost level, and the
reader is Xbound to find one sooner or later. Everything on the level
will be exactly Xas it was when the character died, with one
addition. The old character's Xghost is also on the level. Ghosts,
although hard to kill, can do almost no Xdamage. The nice thing about
ghost levels is that all of the old character's Xitems are in one
pile on the space the character died. It is a simple matter Xto get
these items. But be warned; most of these items will be cursed. XIn
fact, the hardest thing about ghost levels is that usually whatever
Xkilled the old character is powerful enough to kill the new
character as Xwell. There can be multiple ghost on one level. I
recall one level Xwhich had three cockatrices and five ghosts.
X
XFortunately, ghost levels are eventually cleared by the program after
Xa certain period of time, so the user does not have to worry to much
about Xthem in general.
X
XWISHING FOR OBJECTS:
X
XNear the end of the game, the user will find wands of wishing. When
Xthese are zapped, the user is asked to wish for an item. One of the
most Xfrustrating things in hack is when a beginning player can not
figure out Xwhat the exact name of the item he wants to wish for is.
